  "summary": "North Richland Hills, TX, demanded $2.3 million from the Texas Privacy Coalition to fulfill a public records request about police use of Flock ALPR cameras, part of a broader pattern of Texas agencies charging exorbitant fees or denying requests entirely. The article also covers a recent Oklahoma federal ruling that a Flock search was unconstitutional, a Texas ex-cop's guilty plea for misusing Flock data, and Gov. Abbott's halt of Flock funding.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "city officials in a Fort Worth suburb, North Richland Hills, asked one group to pay $2.3 million before it would fulfill a public records request for Flock data",
      "paragraph_idx": 1
    },
    {
      "quote": "searching \"about a terabyte worth of communications about errors, misuse, and effectiveness of the Flock system\" would take approximately 14 years of labor at a rate of $15 per hour",
      "paragraph_idx": 1
    },
    {
      "quote": "Texas Governor Greg Abbott quickly halted Flock funding and stopped issuing permits",
      "paragraph_idx": 5
    },
    {
      "quote": "Flock's network \"is a type of indiscriminate mass surveillance,\" with its ALPR technology creating a \"large-scale, dragnet-type surveillance system\" that should require a warrant to search",
      "paragraph_idx": 8
    },
    {
      "quote": "In Texas, there are hundreds of agencies using Flock cameras, and only 59 agencies use transparency portals... Of those, just 16 share data with the public.",
      "paragraph_idx": 11
    }
  ],
